{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774127708,"nonce":"73P/Jl6FvE3c25g+2pr0DSYpyhc=","title":"Hybrid SDK+Channel: inject channel MCP into SDK sessions","message":"SDK sessions can be simultaneously channel-connected by registering the Mandible channel server as an mcpServers entry in the SDK query options. This gives SDK sessions the channel tools (inbox, work units, auth submission) from the agent perspective, while Mandible retains full SDK control externally.\n\n## Rationale\n- SDK sessions currently access Mandible features via hooks (SessionStart, SubagentStart, etc.)\n- Channel tools provide a richer, agent-initiated interface (mandible_check_inbox, mandible_request_work, etc.)\n- The hybrid is non-redundant: hooks are Mandible-to-agent, channel tools are agent-to-Mandible\n- Together they enable full bidirectional integration\n\n## Implementation\n- SDKBackend.buildQueryOptions() should include mcpServers config pointing to the Mandible channel server\n- The channel server process would be spawned as a sidecar for each SDK session (or a shared instance)\n- Session registration in channel-registry.ts already handles the session_id overlap correctly\n- Need to avoid double-counting: SDK hooks and channel tools should not create redundant session registrations\n\n## Open questions\n- One channel server process per SDK session, or a shared singleton?\n- How to pass the console session ID to the channel server env (MANDIBLE_SESSION_ID)?\n- Does the SDK support mcpServers with stdio transport pointing to a local script?\n- Should channel notifications be suppressed for SDK sessions (since SDK has richer hooks)?\n\n## Dependencies\n- SDK backend (9bf8b4c) — done\n- Channel server (73e0adf) — done\n- @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk mcpServers support","files":null}]}